description:
A lot of the treegraph-related C++ takes the form of something like:

```c++
std::vector < Node* > x;
for(unsigned int i = 0; i < y.size(); i++){
    x.push_back(y[i])
}
```

The problem is that a vector is contiguous memory; every time you push_back() on it you're committing the same anti-patterns you'd get appending to a vector in R (recreating it anew each time). Deques are much the same for these purposes (albeit with a slightly larger memory footprint) but are non-contiguous, so push_back() operations are much much much faster. So I've switched out vectors for deques.

It compiles, although I'm not *entirely* sure if it works since there are no unit tests that I can see and the functions that call the treegraph code don't seem to have examples...
